.env*
*.db
__pycache__/
tests/
docs/

# vercel.json sets includeFiles "**" for the Python function, so anything sitting at the repo
# root is uploaded into the bundle. These hold plaintext redeemable share tokens. They are
# already gitignored, which is exactly why they were easy to miss here — .gitignore keeps them
# out of the repository, and this file is what keeps them out of the deployment.
#
# Measured 2026-09-17: the API project serves no static files — every repo-root path returns
# 404 and only /api/* exists — so nothing was ever reachable over HTTP. This is about what
# travels in the bundle, not about a leak that happened.
joincode*.txt
demo-secrets.txt

# web/ is NOT listed here, and must not be.
#
# It was, for about an hour on 2026-09-17, on the reasoning that the Python function has
# never needed a byte of the front end. That reasoning is right and the conclusion was
# wrong: this file is read by the git builds as well, and fencepost-web builds from this
# same repository with web/ as its Root Directory. Excluding it did not trim the API
# bundle — it deleted the front end's source.
#
# The build still succeeded. Next.js found no app directory, emitted a single 404 page
# through the pages router, and Vercel deployed that. Every route on fencepost-app.vercel.app
# answered 404 while the deployment status read "Ready", which is why nothing shouted.
#
# The lesson is about this file rather than about web/: .vercelignore is shared by every
# project built out of this repository, so an entry that is harmless for one of them can be
# fatal to another. Before adding a path, ask what the OTHER project needs.
